<?xml version="1.0"?>
<launch>
  <!--
    WORLD - default false, and that is deliberate.

    This include used to be unconditional, and there was no `world` arg at all,
    so the `world:=false` that run_campaign.sh passes was silently ignored by
    roslaunch.  The guard its comment describes has therefore never existed:

        "world:=false because the world is already up, above, with this run's
         seed and paused:=false.  Without it the SLAM launch brings its own
         Gazebo and its own copy of every robot node, which unloads the wheel
         controllers."

    That is exactly what was happening, on every campaign run and in the manual
    three-terminal flow in the README too, since both of them start the world
    in a separate terminal first.  The second gzserver dies on the taken port,
    but the second gzclient connects to the live server - which is the second
    Gazebo window - and, more seriously, a SECOND COPY of every node inside the
    robot's namespace comes up with it: spawn_*, controller_starter,
    twist_to_*, robot_state_publisher, and on the Rocker-Bogie the
    rocker_differential node, which would then apply its coupling torque twice.

    false is right because BOTH documented ways of running this bring the world
    up separately.  Pass world:=true for a self-contained SLAM launch.
  -->
  <arg name="world" default="false"/>
  <include file="$(find differential)/launch/lcmine_husky_world.launch" if="$(arg world)"/>

  <arg name="use_sim_time" default="true"/>
  <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(arg use_sim_time)"/>

  <group ns="rtabmap">

  <!-- ICP ODOMETRY -->
  <node pkg="rtabmap_odom" type="icp_odometry" name="icp_odometry" output="screen">
    <remap from="scan_cloud" to="/velodyne_points"/>
    <remap from="imu" to="/imu/data"/>

    <param name="frame_id" type="string" value="husky/base_link"/>
    <param name="odom_frame_id" type="string" value="odom"/>
    <param name="publish_tf" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="wait_for_transform" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="wait_for_transform_duration" type="double" value="0.5"/>

    <!-- IMU CONFIGURATION -->
    <param name="Imu/Enable" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="wait_imu_to_init" type="bool" value="true"/>

    <!-- Sin correccion intra-barrido ni semilla de movimiento externa:
         quitadas el 2026-09-13 porque no se usan.  La nube llega barrida a
         rtabmap, como en todas las campanas. -->

    <!-- GROUND CONSTRAINT -->
    <param name="Odom/AlignWithGround" type="bool" value="true"/>

    <!-- ICP parameters -->
    <param name="Icp/VoxelSize" type="string" value="0.1"/>
    <param name="Icp/PointToPlaneK" type="string" value="20"/>
    <param name="Icp/PointToPlane" type="string" value="true"/>
    <!-- 30 -> 15 EL 2026-09-04, medido.  El tope SI estaba mordiendo pese a
         Icp/Epsilon 0.0001: bajarlo a 15 quita el 36 % del tiempo de registro
         y no mueve la calidad.  Las 15 iteraciones que sobraban refinaban por
         debajo del ruido, o sea que se pagaban y no compraban nada.
         Barrido en ~/barrido_icp, corridas a RTF 0.5 sobre la misma ruta.
         El nodo rtabmap de mas abajo lleva su propio Icp/Iterations, hoy
         en 22 (era 30 hasta el 2026-09-08): ese es el ICP del cierre de
         bucle, corre fuera del presupuesto por nube y ahi las iteraciones
         de mas no cuestan cadencia. -->
    <param name="Icp/Iterations" type="string" value="15"/>
    <param name="Icp/Epsilon" type="string" value="0.0001"/>
    <param name="Icp/MaxTranslation" type="string" value="2.0"/>
    <param name="Icp/MaxCorrespondenceDistance" type="string" value="1.0"/>
    <param name="Icp/PM" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="Icp/PMOutlierRatio" type="string" value="0.85"/>

    <!-- Odometry Strategy -->
    <param name="Odom/Strategy" type="string" value="0"/>
    <param name="Odom/ScanKeyFrameThr" type="string" value="0.9"/>
    <param name="Odom/ResetCountdown" type="string" value="0"/>

    <!-- Frame to Map -->
    <param name="OdomF2M/ScanSubtractRadius" type="string" value="0.1"/>
    <!-- 15000 -> 7500 EL 2026-09-04, medido.  Es el mapa local contra el que
         se registra cada barrido, y era la razon de que el tiempo de ICP
         CRECIESE durante la corrida: +11.2 ms por minuto, de 196 ms al
         arrancar a 330 al final de una vuelta.  A la mitad, el crecimiento
         baja a 2.8 ms/min y con ello el p99, que es lo que decide si una nube
         entra en su presupuesto.

         JUNTO CON Icp/Iterations 15, medido sobre el mismo tramo de 150 s:

             base        med 141  p90 164  p99 192  max 282 ms   ratio 0.755
             las dos     med  65  p90  77  p99  92  max 109 ms   ratio 0.751

         La precision NO cambia: la std que declara la odometria sale 0.0894 m
         en las dos, y el ratio de inliers 0.751 contra 0.755.  Lo unico que
         se va es tiempo.

         QUE COMPRA.  A RTF 0.5 el presupuesto de pared por nube son 203 ms.
         Con el ICP viejo cabia el 36 % de las nubes y rtabmap tiraba el 38 %
         de ellas; con este cabe el 100.0 % y procesa el 99.7 %, medido sobre
         una vuelta entera.  Es lo que permite correr la campaña a RTF 0.5 en
         vez de 0.25, o sea a la mitad de reloj. -->
    <param name="OdomF2M/ScanMaxSize" type="string" value="7500"/>
    <param name="OdomF2M/BundleAdjustment" type="string" value="1"/>
  </node>

  <!-- RTAB-MAP -->
  <node name="rtabmap" pkg="rtabmap_slam" type="rtabmap" output="screen" args="--delete_db_on_start">

    <param name="frame_id" type="string" value="husky/base_link"/>
    <param name="odom_frame_id" type="string" value="odom"/>
    <param name="subscribe_depth" type="bool" value="false"/>
    <param name="subscribe_rgb" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="subscribe_scan_cloud" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="approx_sync" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="queue_size" type="int" value="10"/>

    <remap from="rgb/image" to="/camera/image_raw"/>
    <remap from="rgb/camera_info" to="/camera/camera_info"/>
    <remap from="scan_cloud" to="/velodyne_points"/>
    <remap from="imu" to="/imu/data"/>

    <!-- Database -->
    <param name="database_path" type="string" value="~/.ros/rtabmap_3d.db"/>

    <!-- TF Publishing -->
    <param name="publish_tf" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="map_always_update" type="bool" value="true"/>
    <param name="map_empty_ray_tracing" type="bool" value="true"/>

    <!-- IMU -->
    <param name="Imu/Enable" type="string" value="true"/>

    <!-- Deteccion y memoria -->
    <param name="Rtabmap/DetectionRate" type="string" value="1.0"/>
    <param name="Rtabmap/TimeThr" type="string" value="0"/>
    <param name="Mem/IncrementalMemory" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="Mem/InitWMWithAllNodes" type="string" value="false"/>

    <!-- 3D Mapping -->
    <!-- ADELGAZADO 2026-08-24 (Grid/3D, Grid/CellSize, Grid/RangeMax).
         Antes: 3D, celda 0.05 m, alcance 50 m.  Con eso rtabmap crecia
         ~275 MB/min sin techo: 4693 MB y 242 MB libres de 7738 a los 14
         min, descolgues del SLAM ("Did not receive data") desde el minuto
         5 y el RTF hundiendose de 0.47 a 0.13 por thrashing.  La vuelta
         autonoma se corto en 20 de 43 waypoints.  NINGUNA corrida de la
         campana podia terminar.
         Trazar rayos y guardar ocupacion hasta 50 m a 5 cm de celda, en
         3D, dentro de una galeria cuyas paredes estan a 2-5 m, es casi
         todo espacio vacio.
         Se adelgaza el nodo y NO se limita su numero: Rtabmap/MemoryThr
         chocaria con RGBD/ProximityMaxGraphDepth: 0, que existe para que
         la segunda vuelta cierre contra la primera a ~137 nodos de
         distancia (ver el comentario largo mas abajo).
         Lo que consume la rejilla: el costmap global de move_base lee
         /rtabmap/grid_map, que es la proyeccion 2D.  map_vs_groundtruth.py
         puntua los mapas con la nube exportada de los ESCANEOS
         (rtabmap-export, opcion cloud), no con la rejilla.  OJO: un
         doble guion no puede aparecer dentro de un comentario XML;
         escribirlo aqui hizo que roslaunch rechazara el archivo entero
         y el SLAM no arrancara. -->
         Si se quiere el mapa 3D de vuelta, Grid/3D es el primero a
         revertir, y hay que volver a medir el consumo.
    <param name="Grid/3D" type="string" value="false"/>
    <param name="Grid/FromDepth" type="string" value="false"/>
    <param name="Grid/RayTracing" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="Grid/CellSize" type="string" value="0.10"/>
    <param name="Grid/ClusterRadius" type="string" value="1"/>
    <param name="Grid/GroundIsObstacle" type="string" value="false"/>
    <param name="Grid/MaxObstacleHeight" type="string" value="2.0"/>
    <param name="Grid/MaxGroundHeight" type="string" value="0.0"/>
    <param name="Grid/NormalK" type="string" value="20"/>
    <param name="Grid/RangeMax" type="string" value="15.0"/>
    <param name="Grid/RangeMin" type="string" value="1.0"/>
    <param name="GridGlobal/MinSize" type="string" value="20"/>
    <param name="GridGlobal/Eroded" type="string" value="false"/>

    <!-- ICP for loop closure -->
    <param name="Reg/Strategy" type="string" value="1"/>
    <param name="Icp/VoxelSize" type="string" value="0.1"/>
    <param name="Icp/PointToPlaneK" type="string" value="20"/>
    <param name="Icp/PointToPlane" type="string" value="true"/>
        <!-- 30 -> 22 EL 2026-09-08.  Recorte del 25 % pedido a mano;
         30 * 0.75 = 22.5 y hace falta un entero, asi que 22, que es el
         que cumple el 25 % entero (23 se quedaria en el 23.3 %).

         QUE PESA ESTE NODO.  Medido el 2026-09-08 sobre los slam.log que
         ya estaban en disco (metrics_output_rtf05_3robots y
         metrics_output_rtf025_3robots, las tres plataformas), sin lanzar
         ninguna corrida nueva: los dos nodos imprimen su propio tiempo de
         pared, asi que el reparto sale de contarlo y sumarlo.

             nodo           llamadas   mediana      total     reparto
             icp_odometry   ~10 / s    107-219 ms   409-1125 s   67-72 %
             rtabmap        ~1 / s     504-917 ms   195-493 s    28-33 %

         Este nodo es un tercio del coste del SLAM y su iteracion sale 5x
         mas cara que una nube de odometria; lo que lo salva es que solo
         corre a Rtabmap/DetectionRate = 1.0 Hz.

         LO QUE ESTE RECORTE NO HACE: acelerar la campana.  Con 16 nucleos
         los dos nodos van en paralelo, y quien decide si una nube entra en
         su presupuesto de 203 ms es icp_odometry, no este.  El porcentaje
         de nubes procesadas no se mueve.

         LO QUE NO ESTA MEDIDO: que fraccion de la iteracion es ICP.  El
         desglose que rtabmap imprime es Maps update 13-18 %, pub 1-9 % y
         Conversion ~0 %; el 82-87 % restante mezcla registro, memoria y
         optimizacion del grafo sin separarlos.  O sea que el ahorro de
         este recorte NO se puede calcular desde el log: hay que medirlo.

         Y LA CALIDAD ES LO QUE HAY QUE VIGILAR, no el tiempo: este es el
         ICP que valida los cierres de bucle, y menos iteraciones es menos
         refinamiento en la transformacion que se acepta o se rechaza.  Los
         numeros a mirar son los cierres aceptados y el ATE. -->
    <param name="Icp/Iterations" type="string" value="22"/>
    <!-- RESTORED 2026-08-18.  These four values were uncommitted working-tree
         changes that a `git checkout` of this file destroyed, in all three
         packages.  The rocker_bogie copies were read back out of a live ROS
         parameter server; these two had no running simulation to read from, so
         they are restored from the rocker_bogie values on the study's own
         parity requirement - the SLAM configuration must be identical across
         the three platforms, and these four were the ONLY parameters that
         differed once the frame and topic names are excluded.
           Icp/Epsilon         0.001 -> 0.0001
           Icp/MaxTranslation  3     -> 2.0
           Icp/PMOutlierRatio  0.7   -> 0.85
           Optimizer/Strategy  1     -> 2
         The comments that documented WHY they were chosen are not recoverable.
    -->
    <param name="Icp/Epsilon" type="string" value="0.0001"/>
    <param name="Icp/MaxTranslation" type="string" value="2.0"/>
    <param name="Icp/MaxCorrespondenceDistance" type="string" value="1.0"/>
    <param name="Icp/PM" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="Icp/PMOutlierRatio" type="string" value="0.85"/>

    <!-- Visual features -->
    <param name="Kp/MaxFeatures" type="string" value="400"/>
    <!-- 8 = GFTT/ORB, and Vis/FeatureType is declared HERE so it matches.
         Until 2026-08-23 this was 6 (GFTT/BRIEF) while Vis/FeatureType was
         never declared at all and so kept its default of 8.  rtabmap resolves
         that disagreement by switching a third parameter off:
           "Mem/UseOdomFeatures is enabled, but Vis/FeatureType and
            Kp/DetectorStrategy parameters are not the same! Disabling..."
         The odometry and loop-closure pipelines then extract features
         independently, and every loop-closure candidate is rejected with
           "Not enough features in images (old=0, new=~840, min=20)"
         because the retrieved node comes back without usable words while the
         new one still has its own.  Measured on the two-lap rocker_bogie run
         of 2026-08-23: 37 candidates, 37 rejected, 0 accepted, every one of
         them old=0.  The database showed the features were never the problem
         - all 270 nodes held 846-917 features and their image.
         6 was never really in effect anyway: this OpenCV is built without
         xfeatures2d, so BRIEF falls back to ORB with a warning.  Setting both
         to 8 makes the configuration state what the code already does.
         KEEP THESE TWO EQUAL, and equal across all three platforms. -->
    <param name="Kp/DetectorStrategy" type="string" value="8"/>
    <param name="Vis/FeatureType" type="string" value="8"/>
    <param name="GFTT/MinDistance" type="string" value="10"/>

    <!-- Loop closure -->
    <param name="Mem/NotLinkedNodesKept" type="string" value="false"/>
    <param name="Mem/STMSize" type="string" value="30"/>
    <param name="Rtabmap/LoopRatio" type="string" value="0.7"/>

    <!-- Proximity and loop closure detection -->
    <!-- PROXIMITY DETECTION: two filters in series, and BOTH have to pass.
         Getting only one of them open is what made this look fixed when it
         was not - see the Known issues section of the README.

         RGBD/LocalRadius is a SPATIAL filter, in metres.  20 m rather than
         rtabmap's default of 10 buys margin, because the search runs on the
         ESTIMATED poses: a platform that drifts more than this one needs the
         revisit to still fall inside the radius.  It is NOT what fixes loop
         closure - measured below, with the depth filter open it is worth
         about 4%, which is inside the noise of a single run.

         RGBD/ProximityMaxGraphDepth is a TOPOLOGICAL filter, in graph hops,
         and 0 means "ignore" (rtabmap's own wording).  This is the fix.  At
         50, a candidate more than 50 hops away was discarded BEFORE its
         distance in metres was ever looked at.  One lap of this route creates
         about 137 nodes, so the previous lap's nodes sat 140-200 hops back
         and were rejected on depth alone - no matter how precisely the robot
         came back to the same spot, and no matter how many laps it drove.
         A limit of 50 makes closing ANY loop longer than ~50 nodes
         structurally impossible.  That is why the two-lap runs did not help.

         Measured 2026-08-23, one two-lap rocker_bogie run reprocessed with
         rtabmap-reprocess so every row is the same recorded data:
           depth  radius  closures  ATE rmse  ATE max  links spanning >1/2 lap
              50      20        60     0.792    1.443        0   <- was shipped
               0      10        86     0.177    0.302      172
               0      15       121     0.171    0.325      180
               0      20       150     0.170    0.340      180   <- chosen
         79% lower ATE, and the maximum error falls with the mean - the
         signature of drift being redistributed over the whole graph rather
         than smoothed locally.

         HOW TO CHECK THIS IS REALLY WORKING, because a count does not show
         it: rtabmap reports these as "Prox=N" whether they are revisits or
         merely links to the node that just left short-term memory.  Read the
         Link table of the database instead and look at |from_id - to_id|.
         With the depth limit at 50 every link had a gap of exactly 30 -
         Mem/STMSize - which is local stiffening, not loop closure.  With it
         at 0 the widest are 204<->2 and 205<->4, tying the second lap to the
         first.  That gap is the difference between the two, and the count is
         not.

         The closures are geometric (Prox, not Loop).  Visual loop closure
         cannot work on this robot at all; that is a property of the sensor
         suite, and the README explains it. -->
    <param name="RGBD/LocalRadius" type="string" value="20"/>
    <param name="RGBD/ProximityMaxGraphDepth" type="string" value="0"/>
    <param name="RGBD/ProximityPathMaxNeighbors" type="string" value="10"/>
    <param name="RGBD/AngularUpdate" type="string" value="0.015"/>
    <param name="RGBD/LinearUpdate" type="string" value="0.01"/>
    <param name="RGBD/CreateOccupancyGrid" type="string" value="true"/>
    <param name="Optimizer/Strategy" type="string" value="2"/>
  </node>
  </group>

</launch>
